<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-lysine biosynthesis VI" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5097" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Six pathways are now recognized in bacteria, most algae, fungi and higher plants for the biosynthesis of lysine. They are divided into two groups - the diaminopimelate (DAP) pathways, and the |FRAME:CPD-468| pathways. In the pathways that belong to the DAP group, lysine is produced from aspartate (along with methionine, threonine and isoleucine). All of the DAP group pathways share the upper segments, which include the four steps required for conversion of L-aspartate to |FRAME: DELTA1-PIPERIDEINE-2-6-DICARBOXYLATE|. They also share the last step, which is the conversion of the intermediate |FRAME: MESO-DIAMINOPIMELATE| (D,L-DAP, or &lt;i&gt;meso&lt;/i&gt;-DAP) to lysine. However, these pathways differ in the routes leading from |FRAME: DELTA1-PIPERIDEINE-2-6-DICARBOXYLATE| to |FRAME:MESO-DIAMINOPIMELATE|. The four variations include: (I) the succinylase variant (see |FRAME: DAPLYSINESYN-PWY|), which involves succinylated intermediates. In this route |FRAME: DELTA1-PIPERIDEINE-2-6-DICARBOXYLATE| is coverted to |FRAME:MESO-DIAMINOPIMELATE| in four enzymatic steps. (II) the acetylase variant (see |FRAME: PWY-2941|), which involves acetylated intermediates. This route also involves four enzymatic steps for the conversion of |FRAME: DELTA1-PIPERIDEINE-2-6-DICARBOXYLATE| to |FRAME:MESO-DIAMINOPIMELATE|. (III) the dehydrogenase variant (see |FRAME: PWY-2942|), in which |FRAME: DELTA1-PIPERIDEINE-2-6-DICARBOXYLATE| is converted to |FRAME:MESO-DIAMINOPIMELATE| in a single enzymatic step. (IV) the diaminopimelate-aminotransferase variant (see |FRAME: PWY-5097|), in which |FRAME: DELTA1-PIPERIDEINE-2-6-DICARBOXYLATE| is converted to |FRAME:MESO-DIAMINOPIMELATE| in two steps. In addition to lysine, all of the pathways in this group also produce |FRAME: MESO-DIAMINOPIMELATE|, which is an important metabolite on its own (for example, as a constituent of bacterial cell wall peptidoglycan). The two other pathways belong to the |FRAME: CPD-468| group. They are found in organisms that have no requirement for |FRAME:MESO-DIAMINOPIMELATE|, as it is not a component of their cell walls. These pathways also share the first segments, leading to the synthesis of the intermediate |FRAME: CPD-468| (&amp;alpha;-aminoadipate). However, in yeast and fungi, as well as |FRAME: TAX-3038|, the pathway continues with the key enzyme |FRAME: YBR115C-MONOMER|, leading to the intermediate |FRAME: SACCHAROPINE| (see |FRAME: LYSINE-AMINOAD-PWY|), while in prokaryotes the pathway continues in a path similar to bacterial arginine biosynthesis (see |FRAME: PWY-3081|). Most of the bacteria that have been investigated in detail appear to utilize only one of these pathways |CITS: [1906065]|. A few exceptions include |FRAME: TAX-44252|, in which variants (II) and (III) seem to coexist |CITS: [BARTLETT85]|, and |FRAME: TAX-1718|, in which variant (I) and (III) coexist |CITS: [1906065]|. Lysine is one of the 10 essential amino acids that mammals are unable to synthesize, and must therefore acquire in their diets. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Lysine biosynthesis in plants was thought to be identical to the prokaryotic DAP (diaminopimelate) pathway that uses N-succinylated intermediates (|FRAME: DAPLYSINESYN-PWY|) |CITS: [15652176]|. However the Arabidopsis genome does not contain a homolog of |FRAME: EC-2.3.1.117|. Furthermore, the Arabidopsis homologs of |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.17|, and |FRAME: EC-3.5.1.18|, are not likely to function in lysine biosynthesis |CITS: [15652176]|. It was thus suggested that plants may synthesize lysine via an enzyme that bypasses the three reactions by directly converting tetrahydrodipicolinate to L,L-diaminopimelate. An Arabidopsis enzyme that catalyzes this reaction, |FRAME: AT4G33680-MONOMER|, was indeed discovered |CITS: [16361515]|. Despite the fact that the forward reaction (towards lysine biosynthesis) seems to be unfavorable, the enzyme was shown to be involved in lysine biosynthesis under physiological conditions by functional complementation of an |FRAME: TAX-562| lysine biosynthesis double mutant of dapD and dapE. The key enzyme, |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.83|, was discovered not only in green plants, but also in bacteria belonging to the |FRAME: TAX-1117| and |FRAME: TAX-1783257| |CITS: [17093042]|, and in archaea of the |FRAME: TAX-183925| and the |FRAME: TAX-183939| classes |CITS: [18371309][20418392]|. Soon after it was reported that a diverged form of the enzyme is also common in many other bacterial phyla |CITS: [18310350]|. Phylogenetic analyses suggested that two lateral gene transfers occurred in the DapL genes, one from archaea to bacteria, forming the DapL2 group, and one from bacteria to archaea, forming the DapL1 group |CITS: [20418392]|. The first committed enzyme in this pathway, |FRAME: EC-4.3.3.7|, is feedback regulated by lysine |CITS: [8000011]|. Its catalyzed reaction is the rate-limiting step for lysine biosynthesis. |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.83|, may impose another regulation point of lysine biosynthesis - using maize cultured embryo cells, it was shown that the overall rate of lysine synthesis is in the same range of the forward reaction catalyzed by this enzyme |CITS: [16361515]|. Lysine biosynthesis in plants occurs within chloroplasts. No evidence exists for extra plastidic location of the pathway |CITS: [15652176]|.</Description>
